  "query_preview": "../Saturday through Thursday/ Overall long range \nforecast for the panhandle and gulf waters is generally toward a \nless active pattern. Pacific storm track has shifted farther south\nto start with, with at least one major system going into British \nColumbia early in the weekend. However, less active does not mean \nquiet. Toward the end of the weekend a weakening gale force low \nwill move into the eastern gulf from the S bringing another bout \nof rain and wind (up to gale force in the gulf and some parts of \nthe inner channels) Sunday into early next week. \n\nInto mid week and beyond the pattern turns quieter again as there\nis evidence of a significant ridge building over the area by\nThursday. However, there is no agreement on where exactly that\nridge will be. Some guidance favors the ridge developing over the\neastern gulf with most of the panhandle being dry as a result.\nOther guidance favors a position east of the panhandle with the\nridge being more transient. This scenario favors a more active\neastern gulf with a stalled front pointing a lot of moisture at\nthe N gulf coast late week. In either case, the lower few thousand\nfeet of the atmosphere remains warm enough for any precip to\nremain rain for most Panhandle communities through the middle of\nnext week.",
